417	### 1. Dispatch the implementer
418	
419	Record BASE right after `git switch <branch>` and before dispatching, as the
420	task branch's fork point from the integration branch:
421	`git merge-base HEAD refs/heads/<integration branch>` (the full ref name, so
422	a tag with the branch's name cannot be read instead). On a fresh claim that
423	is the claim's `from=` commit. On a takeover it is the original claim's base,
424	or, once the conflict route has merged the integration branch into the task
425	branch, the integration head merged in; the previous holder's commits stay in
426	the review package and the fix-round diffs either way, which both need BASE.
427	A HEAD read before the claim can lag the integration branch while other
428	sessions merge.
429	
430	- **Task brief:** before dispatching an implementer, run this skill's
431	  `scripts/task-brief PLAN_FILE N` — it extracts the task's full text to a
432	  uniquely named file and prints `wrote <path>: <N> lines`. Take the path
433	  from that line; do not paste the line itself into a dispatch. Compose the
434	  dispatch so the
435	  brief stays the single source of
436	  requirements. Your dispatch should contain: (1) one line on where this
437	  task fits in the project; (2) the brief path, introduced as "read this
438	  first — it is your requirements, with the exact values to use verbatim";
439	  (3) interfaces and decisions from earlier tasks that the brief cannot
440	  know; (4) your resolution of any ambiguity you noticed in the brief;
441	  (5) the report-file path and report contract. Exact values (numbers,
442	  magic strings, signatures, test cases) appear only in the brief. Never
443	  make a subagent read the whole plan file.
444	- **Report file:** name the implementer's report file after the brief
445	  (brief `…/task-N-brief.md` → report `…/task-N-report.md`) and put it in
446	  the dispatch prompt. The implementer writes the full report there and
447	  returns only status, commits, a one-line test summary, and concerns.
448	- A dispatch prompt describes one task, not the session's history. Do not
449	  paste accumulated prior-task summaries ("state after Tasks 1-3") into
450	  later dispatches — a real session's dispatch hit 42k chars of which 99%
451	  was pasted history. A fresh subagent needs its task, the interfaces it
452	  touches, and the global constraints. Nothing else.
453	- The dispatch carries the no-subagents contract (it is in the
454	  implementer template): the implementer never dispatches subagents —
455	  not helpers, and never a reviewer. Review arrives from you, after the
456	  report. In real sessions, every reviewer a worker spawned duplicated
457	  the task review the controller dispatched anyway — a full extra
458	  review seat per task.
459	- Subagents follow hyperpowers:test-driven-development for the task's tests.
460	- If an earlier task deferred a finding in the area this task touches — a
461	  Minor in the ledger, or one your human partner decided — carry a pointer
462	  to that ledger entry in the dispatch.
463	- Ask what to take: `scripts/sdd-state next PLAN_FILE --session <sid>`.
464	  `status=next tasks=<list>` names the claimable tasks in plan order, this
465	  session's own claims first; claim the first:
466	  `scripts/sdd-state claim PLAN_FILE N --session <sid>`. `claimed` prints the
467	  task branch and its base: check the branch out (`git switch <branch>`) and
468	  dispatch on it; `held` with your own session id (`own=1`, a resume after
469	  compaction) prints the same two fields: check the branch out and proceed;
470	  `held` with `own=1` and `merged=1` means the task is already merged and its
471	  branch deleted: check nothing out and resume it at its completion line
472	  (Complete the task, below); `claimed` with `takeover=1` and `merged=1`
473	  means the same after a takeover: the task is already merged, so check
474	  nothing out, dispatch nothing, and resume it at its completion line (the
475	  previous holder's task branch may still exist; you do not need it);
476	  `held` from `claim` after `next` offered the task is a race lost, run
477	  `next` again; `blocked` means the plan moved under you, run `next` again.
478	  A task held by another session is ordinary while next offers another: take
479	  what it offers. When `next` prints `status=none reason=<reason>`:
480	  `complete` goes to the final train (Finish below); `assigned-complete` is
481	  reported and the session hands back; `held` and `blocked` mean post a
482	  waiting record (`scripts/sdd-state wait PLAN_FILE --session <sid> --kind
483	  tasks --note "<the held= and waiting= fields>"`), print
484	  `scripts/sdd-state board PLAN_FILE`, and hand back with the board's session
485	  lines in your message. Take a held task over only on your human partner's
