131	## Setup
132	
133	Read before you settle: run the `sync` and `sdd-dir` steps below and read
134	the ledger's plan line, if the ledger exists (reading it changes nothing).
135	Then settle where the work happens: use hyperpowers:using-git-worktrees,
136	which detects an existing worktree or asks your human partner to choose
137	between staying on the current branch, a new branch in place, and a
138	worktree, and reports the mode. When the plan line names an integration
139	branch, this is a later session of a running plan: hand using-git-worktrees
140	that branch and your session id. Never start implementation on a main/master
141	branch unless your human partner chose "stay on the current branch" in that
142	question; that answer is the consent, and the dossier no longer depends on
143	the branch.
144	
145	Conversation memory does not survive compaction. In real sessions,
146	controllers that lost their place have re-dispatched entire completed task
147	sequences — the single most expensive failure observed. Track progress in
148	a ledger file, not only in todos.
149	
150	- Each plan owns a workspace: at skill start, run `scripts/sdd-state sync PLAN_FILE`
151	  (it fetches the repository's state branch; outcomes below), then this
152	  skill's `scripts/sdd-dir PLAN_FILE` — it prints the plan's workspace
153	  inside the repository's state checkout under the user cache
154	  (`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/hyperpowers/state/<key>/plans/<plan-slug>-<hash8>/`,
155	  one checkout per clone, shared by every worktree of it), home to every
156	  artifact for THIS plan: ledger, briefs, reports, review packages, claims,
157	  tier-skip summary. The ledger, reports, notes and claims are committed to
158	  the orphan branch `hyperpowers/state` by `scripts/sdd-state snapshot PLAN_FILE`
159	  and travel between hosts with that branch; briefs and review packages are
160	  ignored there and regenerate. The cache is off any protected path, so
161	  writing there never triggers a permission prompt, and it is outside the
162	  working tree, so it can never be committed with the code. Another plan's
163	  directory is never yours to read or write. When `sdd-dir` reports
164	  `backend=plain` on stderr, the state branch is unavailable on this host
165	  and the dossier will not travel from it: tell your human partner once and
166	  continue.
167	- `sdd-state sync` outcomes: `up-to-date`, `fast-forwarded`, `merged`,
168	  `ahead`, `created`, `absent`, `no-remote`, `assumed-absent`, `plain`:
169	  continue. `offline` (exit 1): continue on the local checkout and, as soon
170	  as the ledger exists (the next bullet), append
171	  `state: sync offline at <time>` to it. `offline-no-state` (exit 1), or
172	  `needs-sync` from `sdd-dir`: stop; this host has no dossier and cannot
173	  learn whether the remote holds one. Ask your human partner to get online,
174	  or to confirm `scripts/sdd-state sync --assume-absent PLAN_FILE` when they
175	  know no remote dossier exists. `diverged` (exit 3): stop and present both
176	  tips and the printed `merge=` command as a question in the Deciding
177	  Together shape; never merge on your own. `busy` (exit 3): retry `sync`
178	  once after the other session's writes settle, then ask.
179	  `foreign` (exit 2): the branch at the state name is not a hyperpowers
180	  state branch; stop, relay the stderr remedy (set git config
181	  `hyperpowers.stateBranch` to another name), and ask.
182	  `remote-finished plans=<ws,...>` (exit 3): another host finished a plan
183	  whose workspace on this host holds ledger lines that are not on the
184	  branch. When the list names this plan's workspace, stop, show your human
185	  partner the lines this host added (the ledger's tail beyond the last
186	  snapshot), and ask whether to snapshot them (then `sync` again, which
187	  reports `diverged` for their merge) or to discard them. A workspace in
188	  the list that is not this plan's belongs to another plan: report its
189	  name and stop without reading or changing it.
190	  Any other status, `status=error` (exit 2) among them, stops the run until
191	  `sync` succeeds; show its stderr line.
192	- Check for this plan's ledger at `<workspace>/progress.md` — this plan's
193	  workspace only, never a sibling's. The workspace is the plan's identity:
194	  `scripts/sdd-dir` maps a plan to ONE directory whatever path form you
195	  hand it, so a ledger sitting in YOUR workspace is this plan's progress
196	  even when its first line records the path in another form. Compare
197	  repo-relative paths: reduce the plan you were handed to its path relative
198	  to the plan repository's toplevel; a line holding an absolute path matches
199	  when it lies inside this repository and reduces to the same relative path,
200	  or, when it names a path that does not exist on this host (a ledger
201	  written on another machine), when it ends with that relative path. Tasks
202	  with a `Task <N>: complete` line are
203	  DONE — do not re-dispatch them; resume at the first task without one. A
204	  task whose last line is a fix round is mid-loop: resume the loop at the
205	  next round. A task whose last line is its implementer identity is a
206	  dispatch in flight: do not re-dispatch — reconcile that agent first
207	  (list your live children, chase its report), resume it by the recorded
208	  id where your harness allows, and only if it cannot be recovered
209	  dispatch a takeover, recording the takeover's identity beside the
210	  original. The scan only reads: every action it calls for (a resume, a
211	  takeover, the next task) waits until `verify` below has returned `ok`.
212	  A first line that resolves to a genuinely different plan
213	  file means this workspace is corrupt, not that you wandered into a
214	  sibling's: stop and surface it to your human partner rather than
215	  starting fresh on top of another plan's state.
216	- When there is no ledger, create it with its identity as the first line,
217	  `# SDD ledger — plan: <repo-relative plan path>` (never an absolute path:
218	  the ledger travels between hosts), through a no-clobber write so two
219	  sessions that both saw no ledger cannot overwrite each other:
220	  `( set -o noclobber; printf '# SDD ledger — plan: %s\n' <repo-relative plan path> > <workspace>/progress.md )`.
221	  When that write fails because the file now exists, another session
222	  created it first: continue as if you had found it. Whether you created
223	  it or found one, append your session line (with `>>`, never a rewrite):
224	  `session <sid>: mode <current-branch|new-branch|worktree> on <task branch or detached> integrating <integration branch> at <toplevel> started <UTC time>`,
225	  where `<sid>` is `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` on Claude Code and, on a harness
226	  without one, a value you generate once as `$(date -u +%s)-$RANDOM$RANDOM`,
227	  and the mode is what using-git-worktrees reported. Append a fresh session
228	  line whenever you move to a new task branch or detach; your newest line is
229	  your location. When your human partner assigned this session specific
230	  tasks, add `session <sid>: tasks <N,M,...>`. Then, once registered (the
231	  next bullet), run `scripts/sdd-state verify PLAN_FILE` (on a found ledger
232	  this is the check that this host holds the code it relies on): `ok` continues;
233	  anything else stops Setup until `verify` passes again. `code-missing`
234	  names commits the ledger relies on that this checkout lacks, so ask your
235	  human partner to pull the named branch; `wrong-repo` names the repository
236	  the ledger was handed off from, so ask which checkout this work belongs
237	  in; `plan-stale` names the plan repository branch to pull, and you read
238	  the plan again after that pull.
239	- Register the plan before anything else is claimed:
240	  `scripts/sdd-state register PLAN_FILE --session <sid> --integration <branch>`,
241	  where `<branch>` is the branch the branch choice produced (the plan's
242	  integration branch; later sessions read it from the ledger's plan line and
243	  pass the same). `registered` continues; `wrong-branch` means this plan is
244	  already running on another integration branch: switch to it or ask;
245	  `spec-branch-mismatch` means a sibling plan of this spec runs on another
246	  branch: use that branch; `branch-closing` means the plan's branch is being
247	  merged and deleted: wait for your human partner; `detached-head` (exit 11)
248	  means a first registration found the repository the line names on a
249	  detached HEAD, as a host-managed workspace can be, and the plan ref is
250	  named after a branch: ask your human partner for the branch to create
251	  (the integration branch itself when plan and code share one repository),
252	  create it in place with `git -C <the repository the line names> switch -c <name>`,
253	  and register again. Any other status stops Setup until `register`
254	  succeeds; show its stderr line. A later session of a
255	  running plan starts its worktree detached at the integration head
256	  (using-git-worktrees says how) and never checks the integration branch
257	  out; a later session (the ledger already has a plan line naming the
258	  integration branch) that is not already detached at that branch's head
259	  runs `git switch --detach <branch>` before `register`, whatever mode
260	  using-git-worktrees reported (an existing worktree, a native tool's
261	  worktree, or work in place); a worktree that skill created detached is
262	  already there. Before that detach, and before any claim, a later session
263	  compares `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` with the `at <toplevel>` of every
264	  other session's newest session line: a match means you are inside another
265	  session's worktree (a native tool can resume one under the same name), so
266	  do not detach, switch or claim there. Leave it, create your own worktree
267	  with a name that carries your session id (using-git-worktrees, Detaching a
268	  later session), append a fresh session line from it, and detach that one.
269	  When the plan file lives in another repository
270	  than the code, pass `--code-repo <code toplevel>` to every `sdd-state`
271	  call; an omitted option means the current repository, and a wrong one is
272	  `wrong-repo`.
273	- Quote the graph in the ledger: run `scripts/sdd-state deps PLAN_FILE` and
274	  append its lines after the scan table. The scan's conflict rows include
275	  `scripts/sdd-state deps --check PLAN_FILE --ledger <workspace>/progress.md`'s
276	  lines: each `conflict tasks=A,B ...` is a row for your human partner, settled
277	  by an amendment (`scripts/sdd-state amend PLAN_FILE --from <edited copy>
278	  --base <plan= blob> --message <subject>`, with `--accept 'tasks=A,B path=<p>'`
279	  when they accept the overlap; the edited copy is a file outside the
280	  checkout's plan path, a copy in the plan's workspace, never the checked-out
281	  plan file itself, which `amend` refuses) and never by editing the plan in
282	  the worktree alone, because the plan ref is what every session reads.
283	- The ledger is your recovery map: the commits it names exist in git even
284	  when your context no longer remembers creating them. After compaction,
285	  trust the ledger and `git log` over your own recollection.
286	- The workspace is scratch for the working tree and a record on the branch:
287	  the Finish step removes the plan's directory through
288	  `scripts/sdd-state finish PLAN_FILE --session <sid>`, and the branch history keeps it, so
289	  an evidence note can cite `git show <sha>:plans/<ws>/<file>`. Nothing
290	  inside the checkout is reclaimed automatically; `scripts/sdd-state prune --repo <dir>`
291	  lists and removes idle plan directories only when asked. Snapshot the
292	  workspace (`scripts/sdd-state snapshot PLAN_FILE`) after the pre-flight
293	  scan table and before the final review; `scripts/sdd-state complete`
294	  snapshots after every completion line it writes, so a hand-off or a crash
295	  loses at most the task in flight. The completion's durable record is the
296	  snapshot `complete` takes, so that helper's status line decides whether
297	  you go on: `status=complete` continues, `busy` and `error` are retried,
298	  and every other status stops. A snapshot you run by hand answers
299	  `snapshot` (or `plain`, where no state branch exists), which continues;
300	  `none` means what you just wrote is not in the workspace the helper sees,
301	  and `error` (exit 2) means the commit or the ref update failed; both stop
302	  until the snapshot succeeds.
303	  When your human partner asks to
304	  hand the run to another host, run `scripts/sdd-state handoff PLAN_FILE --push`
305	  (the only time the state branch is pushed, never on your own initiative),
306	  report its status line, relay `plan-not-committed` with the commands to
307	  commit the plan, relay `code-dirty` with the listed paths as a question
308	  (only an explicit yes passes `--code-dirty-ok`), relay `detached-head`
309	  by asking for a branch name and creating it in the repository the line
310	  names (`repo=code` or `repo=plan`: `git -C <that repository> switch -c <name>`)
311	  before retrying, relay `shallow-source` (exit 12) the same way (a full
312	  clone, or `git -C <that repository> fetch --unshallow`, since a shallow
313	  code or plan repository cannot record a root), and stop on `plain`
314	  (exit 7: this host has no state branch, so there is no dossier to push),
315	  `scan-failed` (exit 5: the pre-push scan named in `hyperpowers.prePushScan`
316	  rejected the range; show its output and ask) and `push-failed` (exit 1:
317	  the remote moved or could not be reached; run `sync` first, then retry):
318	  none of them is a hand-off, so report nothing as ready to pull.
319	  `snapshot ... push=none` (exit 0) is no hand-off either: the plan
320	  repository has no remote, so the record is committed on this host and
321	  nothing left it; report it as a local snapshot and ask your human partner
322	  for a remote if the dossier has to travel. On
323	  `pushed`, list the branches named in `code=` and, when present, `plan=`
324	  for your human partner to push here and pull there. You push none of
325	  them unless asked.
326	
327	Read the plan once, note its context and Global Constraints, and create a
328	todo per task where your harness surfaces todos — the ledger is the
329	progress record either way; todos mirror it, never replace it. Read the
330	plan's `**Spec:**` header and the spec file it names: the spec is the
331	binding authority the plan argues from, and conflicts inside the plan
332	resolve against it. A plan whose spec is `none` or unreachable gets a
333	ledger line saying so — without a spec, a conflict has no tiebreaker but
334	your human partner.
335	
336	When the plan has a spec, dry-run the decision compiler over it now:
337	`bash <finishing skill's directory>/scripts/decisions-compile <spec> --dry-run`
338	(the finishing-a-development-branch skill, `../finishing-a-development-branch/`
339	relative to this one). Exit 0 confirms the spec's `## Decisions` list parses;
340	its numbered listing goes into the ledger under a `Decisions listing:` line
341	for the extractor's dispatch. Exit 2 for a missing list is the legacy-spec
342	case: draft the list from the spec's own text, one item per decision it
343	states, show it to your human partner, and write it into the spec once they
344	approve it, committing that edit on the branch. Any other exit 2 names what
345	to fix first (a spec that is not committed, or a `Supersedes:` problem) to
346	resolve with them the same way. After any repair, run the dry run again
347	until it exits 0, and only then write its numbered listing into the ledger.
348	The session is at its start, so this costs no cycle time. When the plan has
349	no spec (`none`, or unreachable, per the ledger line above), write
350	`Decisions: none (no spec)` in the ledger and skip the listing, the
351	extractor and the spec in the hand-off: finishing then takes its test-only
352	path.
353	
354	Before dispatching Task 1, scan the plan once for conflicts, writing down
355	what you checked as you check it:
356	
357	- tasks that contradict each other or the plan's Global Constraints
358	- anything the plan explicitly mandates that the review rubric treats as a
359	  defect (a test that asserts nothing, verbatim duplication of a logic block)
360	
361	The scan's output is a table, not a verdict. One row for every pair of tasks
362	that share a file or an interface: the two tasks, what one produces against
363	what the other consumes, and what you found. One row for every task: whether
364	its own text agrees with itself — the tests it specifies against the code it
365	specifies, the files it creates against the files it later touches. "The scan
366	is clean" without those rows is not a scan you ran.
367	
368	Write the table to the ledger. Then present everything it surfaced to your
369	human partner as ONE batched question — each finding beside the plan text
370	that mandates it, asking which governs — before execution begins, not one
371	interrupt per discovery mid-plan. Each finding is a decision with
372	consequences downstream, so it gets the shape the brainstorming skill's
373	Deciding Together section describes: the conflict and what each resolution
374	costs in chat, then the selection widget to collect the answers. If the scan
375	is clean, proceed without comment. The review loop remains the net for
376	conflicts that only emerge from implementation.
