378	## Model Selection
379	
380	Match the model to the task's difficulty rather than defaulting. Two rules are
381	defaults you will get wrong by omission, so they stay here: **always specify
382	the model explicitly when dispatching a subagent** — an omitted model inherits
383	your session's, usually the most capable and most expensive — and **a mid-tier
384	model is the floor for reviewers and for implementers working from prose
385	descriptions**, whatever the schedule pressure. The cheapest tier is for
386	transcription, where the task's own text carries the complete code to write,
387	and for single-file mechanical fixes. The per-role guidance, the complexity
388	signals, and the fix-loop escalation rule are in
389	[model-selection.md](model-selection.md).
390	
391	## The Task Loop
392	
393	**Batch small same-shape work.** When the plan lists several tasks that are
394	each a small, independent edit of the same kind — the same one-line fix,
395	constant change, or field addition repeated across files — do not dispatch
396	one subagent per task. Compose ONE dispatch brief listing every file and
397	its change, send the whole batch to a single subagent, and review its diff
398	as one unit. Reserve one-dispatch-per-task for work that needs its own
399	judgment, its own tests, or its own review surface.
400	A batch's effective risk tier for the per-task Codex gate is the MAX of the
401	batched tasks' declared tiers — batching low-tier tasks never manufactures
402	a gate skip.
403	
404	Everything you paste into a dispatch prompt — and everything a subagent
405	prints back — stays resident in your context for the rest of the session
406	and is re-read on every later turn. Hand artifacts over as files.
407	
408	**Waiting on subagents and long commands.** On Claude Code, wait in the foreground:
409	
410	- Dispatch implementer, task-reviewer, fix-round, re-review and final-reviewer subagents with `run_in_background: false`. Your next step always depends on the result, and a foreground call keeps your turn open, which is what an editor such as Zed shows as the session working. A background dispatch ends the turn while the subagent runs, and the editor shows the session idle.
411	- Run a covering command expected to finish within 10 minutes in the foreground, with an explicit timeout of at least its expected runtime and at most 600000 ms.
412	- A command that may run past 10 minutes is a tracked job. Allocate it in the foreground with `scripts/tracked-job new PLAN_FILE <name> --budget-ms <budget>` (the watch budget below; the helper writes the `jobdir=` line in the ledger itself), launch `scripts/tracked-job run <jobdir> -- <command>` through the tracked background option with the budget as its timeout, then watch it in the foreground with repeated `scripts/tracked-job wait <jobdir> --max-ms 600000` calls: `exited code=0` is success; any other `exited` code is a failed command, handled as that command's failure (a failed test is a fix round, a failed covering command a failed task); `running` at the bound means another `wait`; on `lost`, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` first and read its answer before you go on: only `stopped` or `not-running` means the survivors are swept and the job can be treated as failed (a process in the job's group that carried no fence is left alone and counted as `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, and one that outlived the KILL is counted in `survivors`; report a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped`, or a record you cannot read, to your human partner with the jobdir and pgid before you go on, and never call that job stopped); `busy` means the lock was held and nothing was swept, so run `stop` again; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it to your human partner naming the jobdir and leave the job live; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` means the sweep needs a shell that can list processes and read their identity (the sandbox cannot), so the job stays live to the stop guard until `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` runs where it can: report it to your human partner with its pgid beside the failure, and expect a reused pid to read as `running` until that sweep. When you need a job's output, write the tail of its `out.log` to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and cite the path; output never goes in the ledger. A background shell you start in a turn without `tracked-job` is live work no job record names: end the turn while one is still running and the stop guard refuses that turn end as `live-shell`. Codex reviews already follow this shape through the recipe's `status <job-id> --wait --json` loop.
413	- **Watch budget.** The total watch on one job is three times the plan's stated runtime for it, or 60 minutes when the plan states none, capped at the harness's maximum background lifetime (7200000 ms on Claude Code). The watch ends when `wait` reports `exited`, `lost` or `lost-unswept`; a supervisor the harness killed writes no exit file, and `wait` reports it as `lost`. When the budget is spent with the job still running, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` and read its answer before you end the turn: `stopped` or `not-running` means the job is over, so write the `out.log` tail to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and report BLOCKED naming that path (a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, or a record you cannot read, is reported the same way, with the jobdir and pgid, and that job is never called stopped); `busy` means the workspace lock was held and nothing changed, so run `stop` again; `stop-timeout` means the supervisor has not concluded the job, so run `stop` again, three calls in all, and escalate a third one to your human partner as a job still live, naming the jobdir; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it the same way, naming the jobdir; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` leaves the job live, so report it to your human partner as still live with its pgid, never as stopped.
414	
415	On a harness whose subagents run asynchronously, never poll a wait interface with short timeouts, and never sit in one silent, open-ended wait either. While you have local work — ledger updates, packaging the next review, reading reports — keep working; child results arrive on their own. When you are genuinely idle, wait in bounded stretches (five to ten minutes, where your platform allows), and between stretches post one line of status and reconcile your live children: list them, and chase any that finished without reporting. A bounded stretch keeps nearly all of a long wait's efficiency while guaranteeing a stuck or lost child is noticed within minutes, not at the end of the session. Codex's `wait_agent` blocks inside the turn; `using-hyperpowers/references/codex-tools.md` maps it.
416	
417	### 1. Dispatch the implementer
418	
419	Record BASE right after `git switch <branch>` and before dispatching, as the
420	task branch's fork point from the integration branch:
421	`git merge-base HEAD refs/heads/<integration branch>` (the full ref name, so
422	a tag with the branch's name cannot be read instead). On a fresh claim that
423	is the claim's `from=` commit. On a takeover it is the original claim's base,
424	or, once the conflict route has merged the integration branch into the task
425	branch, the integration head merged in; the previous holder's commits stay in
426	the review package and the fix-round diffs either way, which both need BASE.
427	A HEAD read before the claim can lag the integration branch while other
428	sessions merge.
429	
430	- **Task brief:** before dispatching an implementer, run this skill's
431	  `scripts/task-brief PLAN_FILE N` — it extracts the task's full text to a
432	  uniquely named file and prints `wrote <path>: <N> lines`. Take the path
433	  from that line; do not paste the line itself into a dispatch. Compose the
434	  dispatch so the
435	  brief stays the single source of
436	  requirements. Your dispatch should contain: (1) one line on where this
437	  task fits in the project; (2) the brief path, introduced as "read this
438	  first — it is your requirements, with the exact values to use verbatim";
439	  (3) interfaces and decisions from earlier tasks that the brief cannot
440	  know; (4) your resolution of any ambiguity you noticed in the brief;
441	  (5) the report-file path and report contract. Exact values (numbers,
442	  magic strings, signatures, test cases) appear only in the brief. Never
443	  make a subagent read the whole plan file.
444	- **Report file:** name the implementer's report file after the brief
445	  (brief `…/task-N-brief.md` → report `…/task-N-report.md`) and put it in
446	  the dispatch prompt. The implementer writes the full report there and
447	  returns only status, commits, a one-line test summary, and concerns.
448	- A dispatch prompt describes one task, not the session's history. Do not
449	  paste accumulated prior-task summaries ("state after Tasks 1-3") into
450	  later dispatches — a real session's dispatch hit 42k chars of which 99%
451	  was pasted history. A fresh subagent needs its task, the interfaces it
452	  touches, and the global constraints. Nothing else.
453	- The dispatch carries the no-subagents contract (it is in the
454	  implementer template): the implementer never dispatches subagents —
455	  not helpers, and never a reviewer. Review arrives from you, after the
456	  report. In real sessions, every reviewer a worker spawned duplicated
457	  the task review the controller dispatched anyway — a full extra
458	  review seat per task.
459	- Subagents follow hyperpowers:test-driven-development for the task's tests.
460	- If an earlier task deferred a finding in the area this task touches — a
461	  Minor in the ledger, or one your human partner decided — carry a pointer
462	  to that ledger entry in the dispatch.
463	- Ask what to take: `scripts/sdd-state next PLAN_FILE --session <sid>`.
464	  `status=next tasks=<list>` names the claimable tasks in plan order, this
465	  session's own claims first; claim the first:
466	  `scripts/sdd-state claim PLAN_FILE N --session <sid>`. `claimed` prints the
467	  task branch and its base: check the branch out (`git switch <branch>`) and
468	  dispatch on it; `held` with your own session id (`own=1`, a resume after
469	  compaction) prints the same two fields: check the branch out and proceed;
470	  `held` with `own=1` and `merged=1` means the task is already merged and its
471	  branch deleted: check nothing out and resume it at its completion line
472	  (Complete the task, below); `claimed` with `takeover=1` and `merged=1`
473	  means the same after a takeover: the task is already merged, so check
474	  nothing out, dispatch nothing, and resume it at its completion line (the
475	  previous holder's task branch may still exist; you do not need it);
476	  `held` from `claim` after `next` offered the task is a race lost, run
477	  `next` again; `blocked` means the plan moved under you, run `next` again.
478	  A task held by another session is ordinary while next offers another: take
479	  what it offers. When `next` prints `status=none reason=<reason>`:
480	  `complete` goes to the final train (Finish below); `assigned-complete` is
481	  reported and the session hands back; `held` and `blocked` mean post a
482	  waiting record (`scripts/sdd-state wait PLAN_FILE --session <sid> --kind
483	  tasks --note "<the held= and waiting= fields>"`), print
484	  `scripts/sdd-state board PLAN_FILE`, and hand back with the board's session
485	  lines in your message. Take a held task over only on your human partner's
486	  explicit yes to a question that names the holder and the time since
487	  (`--takeover`), recording the takeover beside the original identity;
488	  `own-blocked` means an amendment put a prerequisite under your own claim:
489	  post, print the board, hand back, and let your human partner remove the
490	  edge or let the prerequisite complete. `done`, `no-workspace` and `error`
491	  keep their handling: `done`: another session completed the task since you
492	  read the ledger; read its completion line, mark the task done, and never
493	  dispatch it. `no-workspace` (exit 2): the workspace is gone (finished or
494	  pruned elsewhere); stop, run `sync`, and re-read the ledger before
495	  anything else. `error` (exit 2, the same exit as `no-workspace` and a
496	  different problem): a read or a write failed, and the claim may already
497	  have been published; only your own retry of the same claim (which then
498	  answers `held own=1`) or a takeover clears it. Stop, show the stderr line,
499	  and retry the same claim once its cause is fixed; `sync` is still not the
500	  answer. Any other status stops the dispatch until `claim` succeeds; show
501	  its stderr line.
502	- Record the implementer's agent identity from the dispatch result in the
503	  ledger's task entry
504	  (`Task <N>: implementer <agent-id-or-name> (model <model>, session <sid>, worktree <path>, branch <name>)`) —
505	  fix-loop rounds 1-3 resume this agent where the harness has no foreground
506	  dispatch and dispatch on its recorded model where it does, and after compaction the ledger is
507	  the only place the identity survives once its agent has exited. An
508	  identity that was never written down forces a fresh takeover where a
509	  resume was owed.
510	- When another session may be live on this plan, append ledger lines with a
511	  quoted heredoc (`cat >> <workspace>/progress.md <<'EOF'`), never with the
512	  Edit tool, whose read-modify-write drops a line another session appended
513	  in between.
514	- One implementer at a time in this session: parallel work is other sessions' (one controller per task branch); never dispatch two implementers at once here.
515	- Where the Agent tool offers `run_in_background`, dispatch with it false; where it does not (fork mode in an interactive terminal), dispatches run in the background and the stop guard treats them as live work.
