519	### 2. Handle the report
520	
521	**Subagent Reports Are Claims.** Subagent reports are claims, not
522	evidence. Before acting on any completed-status report (DONE or DONE_WITH_CONCERNS) or a fix report —
523	dispatching the reviewer, re-running a gate, marking a task complete — the
524	controller re-runs the named covering test command directly and compares
525	the output against the report. A misreported result is a failed task:
526	re-dispatch with the discrepancy named, not a bookkeeping correction.
527	
528	Every implementer and fix dispatch names either its covering test
529	command(s) or an explicit `no covering command: <rationale>` line plus the
530	controller's substitute verification (read the diff against the brief;
531	render or grep the changed doc). A dispatch naming neither is malformed —
532	fix the dispatch, not the rule.
533	
534	A covering command must be able to fail. One that can no-op on the state
535	under test — a changed-files linter on a clean tree, a test filter that
536	matches nothing — is not covering evidence, however honestly it exits 0.
537	Name the files or the test ids explicitly so the command exercises what
538	the report claims.
539	
540	Implementer subagents report one of four statuses. Handle each appropriately:
541	
542	**DONE:** Generate the review package (`scripts/review-package PLAN_FILE BASE HEAD`, from this skill's directory — it prints `wrote <path>: <N> commit(s), <N> bytes`, so take the path from that line; BASE is the commit you recorded before dispatching the implementer — never `HEAD~1`, which silently drops all but the last commit of a multi-commit task). If `review-package` exits non-zero, read its stderr and fix the range before dispatching — exit 2 means bad inputs (wrong argument count, a missing plan file, a ref that does not resolve) or an ancestry check git itself could not answer (a missing or corrupt object); exit 3 means BASE..HEAD is empty or HEAD is not a descendant of BASE, usually a wrong-branch commit; never dispatch a reviewer without a package. Otherwise, dispatch the task reviewer with the path.
543	
544	**DONE_WITH_CONCERNS:** The implementer completed the work but flagged doubts. Read the concerns before proceeding. If the concerns are about correctness or scope, address them before review. If they're observations (e.g., "this file is getting large"), note them and proceed to review.
545	
546	**NEEDS_CONTEXT:** The implementer needs information that wasn't provided. Provide the missing context and re-dispatch.
547	
548	**BLOCKED:** The implementer cannot complete the task. Assess the blocker:
549	1. If it's a context problem, provide more context and re-dispatch with the same model
550	2. If the task requires more reasoning, re-dispatch with a more capable model
551	3. If the task is too large, break it into smaller pieces
552	4. If the plan itself is wrong, stop and escalate to your human partner — the plan text, what it breaks, and the correction you would make
553	
554	**Never** ignore an escalation or force the same model to retry without changes. If the implementer said it's stuck, something needs to change.
555	
556	If the implementer asks questions — before starting or mid-task — answer
557	clearly and completely, provide additional context if needed, and don't
558	rush it into implementation.
559	
560	### 3. Review the task
561	
562	Per-task reviews are task-scoped gates. The broad review happens once, at the
563	final whole-branch review. Never skip the task review, and never accept a
564	report missing either verdict — spec compliance AND task quality are both
565	required. Implementer self-review never replaces the task review; both are
566	needed. Note the task's effective risk tier as you go (see Risk Tiers,
567	below) — the Claude task review always runs, but the tier decides whether
568	step 4's per-task Codex gate does.
569	
570	- Hand the reviewer its diff as a file: run this skill's
571	  `scripts/review-package PLAN_FILE BASE HEAD` — it prints
572	  `wrote <path>: <N> commit(s), <N> bytes`, so take the path from that line
573	  and pass it to the reviewer (or, without bash: `git log --oneline`,
574	  `git diff --stat`, and `git diff -U10` for the range, redirected to one
575	  uniquely named file). The output never enters your own context, and the reviewer sees
576	  the commit list, stat summary, and full diff with context in one Read
577	  call. Use the BASE you recorded before dispatching the implementer —
578	  never `HEAD~1`, which silently truncates multi-commit tasks. Never
579	  dispatch a task reviewer without a diff file.
580	- **Reviewer inputs:** the task reviewer gets three paths — the same brief
581	  file, the report file, and the review package — plus the global
582	  constraints that bind the task. Write those global constraints to a small
583	  file alongside the brief and report: the per-task Codex gate takes the
584	  same file as its global-constraints handoff.
585	- The global-constraints block you hand the reviewer is its attention
586	  lens. Copy the binding requirements verbatim from the plan's Global
587	  Constraints section or the spec: exact values, exact formats, and the
588	  stated relationships between components ("same layout as X", "matches
589	  Y"). The reviewer's template already carries the process rules (YAGNI,
590	  test hygiene, review method) — the constraints block is for what THIS
591	  project's spec demands.
592	- Do not add open-ended directives like "check all uses" or "run race tests
593	  if useful" without a concrete, task-specific reason
594	- Do not ask a reviewer to re-run tests the implementer already ran on the
595	  same code — the implementer's report carries the test evidence
596	- Do not pre-judge findings for the reviewer — never instruct a reviewer to
597	  ignore or not flag a specific issue. If you believe a finding would be a
598	  false positive, let the reviewer raise it and adjudicate it in the review
599	  loop. If the prompt you are writing contains "do not flag," "don't treat X
600	  as a defect," "at most Minor," or "the plan chose" — stop: you are
601	  pre-judging, usually to spare yourself a review loop. When the plan supplies
602	  example code, weaknesses in that code are fair review targets — the example
603	  was a starting point, not evidence that its weaknesses were chosen.
604	The task reviewer may report "⚠️ Cannot verify from diff" items — requirements
605	that live in unchanged code or span tasks. These do not block the rest of the
606	review, but you must resolve each one yourself before marking the task
607	complete: you hold the plan and cross-task context the reviewer
608	lacks. If you confirm an item is a real gap, treat it as a failed spec
609	review — it enters the fix loop with the other findings.
610	
611	Template: [task-reviewer-prompt.md](task-reviewer-prompt.md)
612	
613	### 4. The fix loop
614	
615	The loop triggers when the review reports spec ❌, any Critical or Important
616	finding, a ⚠️ item you confirmed as a real gap, or a blocking finding from
617	the per-task Codex gate below.
618	
619	Task-reviewer findings are claims too. The fixing implementer verifies
620	each finding against the code before fixing it
621	(hyperpowers:receiving-code-review); a finding is declined only as
622	refuted or corrected with file:line evidence, which the controller
623	records in the ledger; a confirmed finding is fixed or carried open; a
624	finding nobody can settle stays open and counts against the round cap.
625	
626	Before the loop starts, two routes leave it immediately:
627	
628	- Record Minor findings in the progress ledger as you go
629	  (`Task <N>: minor (deferred): <one-liner>`), and point the final
630	  whole-branch review at that list so it can triage which must be fixed
631	  before merge. A roll-up nobody reads is a silent discard. Minor findings
632	  never enter the loop.
633	- A finding labeled plan-mandated — or any finding that conflicts with what
634	  the plan's text requires — is your human partner's decision: present the
635	  finding and the plan text beside it and ask which governs. Do not dismiss
636	  the finding because the plan mandates it, and do not dispatch a fix that
637	  contradicts the plan without asking.
638	
639	Everything else enters the loop — the Claude task reviewer's blocking
640	findings and the per-task Codex gate's blocking findings alike. They are ONE
641	loop with ONE shared cap of **five rounds per task**: the gate does not run
642	a count of its own. A fix round is one fix dispatch — or one
643	controller-applied de-minimis fix — plus one scoped re-review.
644	
645	**Rounds 1-3 — the same model, carrying the task's record.** Where the Agent
646	tool offers `run_in_background` (Claude Code outside fork mode), dispatch a
647	fresh implementer in the foreground (`run_in_background: false`) with the
648	brief path, the report-file path, and the open findings verbatim, on the
649	original implementer's model (the `model` in its ledger identity line);
650	tell it a prior implementer did the
651	task and the report file records what it did and tried. A foreground
652	dispatch keeps your turn open until the fix returns, so a fix round on
653	Claude Code dispatches a fresh foreground implementer; a SendMessage resume
654	is for harnesses without the option, Claude Code's fork mode among them. On
655	those harnesses, resume the original
656	implementer with the findings verbatim: its context is intact. If the
657	harness cannot message a live subagent either, dispatch a fresh implementer
658	with the brief path, the report-file path, and the findings; the report file
659	is the persistent memory either way.
660	
661	**Rounds 4-5 — dispatch a fresh takeover implementer on a more capable
662	model** (per Model Selection), with the brief path, the report-file path,
663	the open findings, and this framing: "A prior implementer attempted this
664	task [N] times; you own it now. Read the report file for what was tried." A
665	loop that survives three rounds usually means the same model cannot see
666	its own problem: the takeover brings a more capable one.
667	
668	**Every round, either way:** the implementer fixes, re-runs the tests
669	covering the amended code, appends its fix report to the same report file,
670	and returns the short contract. Before re-dispatching the reviewer, confirm
671	the fix report contains the covering tests, the command run, and the
672	output; dispatch the re-review once all three are present. That
673	precondition covers the findings the round fixed; a round that declines
674	every finding is defined below. Name the covering test files in the fix
675	message — a one-line fix does not need the whole suite. The per-task loop
676	gives a fix round to an implementer rather than a fixer, so it never uses
677	[fix-subagent-prompt.md](fix-subagent-prompt.md) — that template belongs to
678	the final review's one fix wave.
679	
680	**The re-review is scoped.** Run `scripts/review-package PLAN_FILE FIX_BASE HEAD`
681	where FIX_BASE is the head the previous review saw, and dispatch
682	[re-review-prompt.md](re-review-prompt.md) with the findings list, the
683	brief, the report file, and the printed diff path. The re-reviewer verdicts
684	each finding ADDRESSED, NOT ADDRESSED, or DECLINED — a refuted or corrected
685	finding whose file:line evidence the re-reviewer has confirmed; an unconfirmed
686	decline stays NOT ADDRESSED — and flags new breakage in the fix diff only. The
687	controller records each DECLINED finding in the ledger with its evidence. New
688	Critical/Important breakage in the fix diff joins the open findings list.
689	Out-of-scope observations go to the ledger as deferred minors — they never
690	extend the loop. Every round in this loop ends with the scoped re-review —
691	never a full task-reviewer re-run, whatever the finding's origin.
692	
693	**A round that declines every finding changes no code.** There is no fix
694	diff and no covering test to confirm; the implementer's report is the
695	artifact, carrying for each finding the refuted or corrected evidence at
696	file:line. Skip `scripts/review-package` for that round — its range is
697	empty — and dispatch the scoped re-review with the findings list, the brief,
698	the report, and the previous review's package path, so the re-reviewer
699	verdicts each finding DECLINED or NOT ADDRESSED from the evidence alone. The
700	covering-tests precondition applies only to findings that were fixed. Record
701	the round as `(0 addressed, <Y> declined, <Z> open)`; a NOT ADDRESSED verdict
702	keeps that finding open and the round counts toward the cap as usual.
703	
704	**After each round,** append to the ledger:
705	`Task <N>: fix round <R>/5 (<X> addressed, <Y> declined, <Z> open — <finding one-liners>; commits <a7>..<b7>)`
706	
707	Never fix findings yourself in the controller session — your context stays
708	clean for coordination, and controller fixes skip review. One narrow
709	exception: a fix fully specified by the finding itself — exact file, exact
710	lines, exact replacement, no judgment left — touching at most 3 lines in
711	one file with no new logic, may be controller-applied — one finding per
712	reach; a round holding two such findings is not de minimis. The exception
713	waives nothing else: it consumes a fix round and ends in the same scoped
714	re-review; the controller applies the edit and runs the fix's covering
715	command FIRST — a failure means the fix was not de minimis: revert the
716	edit, spend no round, and go back to the round's own rule — then commits
717	the verified fix (the re-review packages committed diffs — an
718	uncommitted fix hands it nothing) and appends the fix report — the
719	command, its output, and a diff summary — to the task's report file
720	itself, exactly as an implementer would. Its ledger line keeps the
721	fix-round schema with the marker inside it:
722	`Task <N>: fix round <R>/5 controller-applied (de minimis) (<X> addressed, <Y> declined, <Z> open — <finding one-liners>; commits <a7>..<b7>)`.
723	Reaching for it twice in the same task means the findings are not de
724	minimis — go back to the round's own rule: hand the findings to an
725	implementer at rounds 1-3, dispatch the takeover at rounds 4-5.
726	
727	**Codex Review Gate (Claude Code only).** When running under Claude Code, add
728	a Codex **code** review gate at two points — here, per task, and once more
729	at the final whole-branch review — following
730	[../requesting-code-review/codex-review-gate.md](../requesting-code-review/codex-review-gate.md).
731	Probe once per skill run and reuse the result; if Codex is absent, emit the
732	no-Codex notice once and run both gates as no-ops.
733	
734	- **Per task:** after the task reviewer approves (spec ✅ and quality approved) and
735	  before marking the task complete, run the gate (standard/high effective tiers; an effective-low task skips it per Risk Tiers below, recording the tier-skip event instead) using the per-task code recipe
736	  with `--base <the task BASE you recorded before dispatching the implementer>`.
737	  Provide the task brief path, implementer report path, review-package path, and
738	  the global-constraints file you wrote in step 3. The gate's dossier `--adjudications` inputs carry the plan's `**Spec:**` file path beside any spec-gate ledger, so the reviewer is handed the binding authority instead of inferring it.
739	- **Rounds are shared, not stacked.** Blocking findings from this gate enter
740	  the same five-round loop above, at the next round number — the gate has no
741	  separate Codex ceiling of its own. The task's fix-round budget is five,
742	  shared across Claude-reviewer rounds and Codex-gate rounds. Three mechanics
743	  keep that single budget honest:
744	
745	  1. **One `GATE_DIR` for this task's whole gate lifecycle.** Create it at
746	     the task's first gate invocation and reuse that same dir for every
747	     Codex re-round of this task — the gate doc's §3 contract, "Use one
748	     `GATE_DIR` for the whole gate." `gate-round`'s counter lives in that
749	     dir, so it counts exactly this task's gate rounds and nothing else.
750	     When the `GATE_DIR` is first created, record it in the progress ledger
751	     as one line `Task N gate dir: <path>`; on resume (or any later Codex
752	     re-round), reuse the ledgered path instead of creating a new one; if
753	     the ledgered dir no longer exists (cache purged), treat the task's
754	     prior gate rounds as unknown and fail closed: do not assume zero —
755	     reconstruct the consumed-round count from the ledger's fix-round
756	     entries and, if gate rounds cannot be established, surface via BLOCKED
757	     rather than restarting the count.
758	  2. **Let the counter do the arithmetic.** `gate-round` compares its LOCAL
759	     count — gate rounds only, monotonic within the `GATE_DIR` — against a
760	     ceiling, so the ceiling must leave gate rounds out; they are already in
761	     that count. Pass `--consumed <NON-gate fix rounds this task has consumed
762	     so far>` (all fix/re-review rounds, whatever the finding's origin — the
763	     gate's own invocation rounds are excluded only because `gate-round`'s
764	     counter already holds them) and the script derives the ceiling from the
765	     shared five-round cap itself. Recompute the consumed count at each call:
766	     non-gate rounds may land between gate rounds. Do not hand-compute
767	     `--ceiling` for a task gate — that is how twenty measured task gates
768	     recorded ceilings of 1, 2, 6, and 7, two of which the cap makes
769	     impossible. `--consumed` cannot express them.
770	  3. **A spent cap is BLOCKED, not a gate round.** When the task's consumed
771	     rounds already total five, `--consumed 5` yields a ceiling of zero and
772	     the gate backstops on its first call. That is the fail-closed floor, not
773	     the intended path: check before calling, and when the cap is spent follow
774	     the breaker below and surface the task as BLOCKED rather than spending a
775	     gate invocation to learn it.
776	
777	  The invariant in one line: **local gate rounds + non-gate fix rounds ≤ 5**,
778	  enforced because `gate-round` blocks (verdict `backstop`) the moment its
779	  local count would exceed the ceiling. Count every round the
780	  gate runs against the task's shared budget — each gate round consumes one
781	  of the five, exactly like a reviewer round. After a Codex-triggered fix,
782	  the scoped re-review verifies it; the gate re-runs only once that
783	  re-review verdicts every finding ADDRESSED or DECLINED.
784	
785	**The breaker.** When round 5's re-review — or a per-task Codex gate at the
786	spent cap — still leaves blocking findings open, stop dispatching. The task
787	does not silently continue and nothing is parked on your own authority:
788	surface the open findings to your human partner as BLOCKED, each with the
789	rounds already spent, what was tried, and the evidence. Ledger it:
790	`Task <N>: BLOCKED at cap — <finding one-liners>`. They decide what happens
791	next.
792	
793	### 5. Complete the task
794	
795	When the review comes back clean — the task reviewer approved, every
796	fix-loop finding is addressed or declined, and the per-task Codex gate
797	approved or was skipped by tier — in this order, each step idempotent on a
798	resume:
799	
800	- Append `Task <N>: approved <full id>` with the head the reviewers saw,
801	  written with `git rev-parse <head>` (every later approval of the same task
802	  appends a new line; the newest is what merges). merge-back compares the
803	  whole id, so a shortened id reads as no approval.
804	- Merge back: `scripts/sdd-state merge-back PLAN_FILE N --session <sid> --commit <sha>`.
805	  `merged` continues (`already=1` on a resume means the ref was moved
806	  before the crash). `conflict paths=<list>`: merge the integration branch
807	  into the task branch in your worktree (`git merge <integration>`), resolve
808	  through one implementer dispatch whose brief names the paths and both
809	  sides, re-run the task reviewer over the resolution and the task gate's
810	  re-review lens, append the new `approved` line, and retry. `checked-out`
811	  names a worktree or `lease:<sid>`: a final train holds the branch; post a
812	  `tasks` record and retry after it ends, or ask your human partner to move
813	  the checkout. `not-approved approved=<sha7|none>` means your bookkeeping
814	  is behind: the commit you passed is not the task's newest `approved` line,
815	  so append the `approved` line for the head the reviewers saw (or pass that
816	  head) and retry. `blocked waiting=<list>` (exit 7) means an amendment put a
817	  prerequisite under your claim after you took it, and merge-back enforces
818	  the edge: leave the `approved` line as it is, and never write a completion
819	  line for a task this session did not complete. Run `next` and take what it
820	  offers; on `own-blocked`, post, print the board and hand back as the
